Q21. Alert box when button is clicked
Answer:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>JavaScript Alert</title>
</head>
<body>

    <button onclick="showMessage()">Click Me</button>

    <script>
        function showMessage() {
            alert("Welcome to JavaScript!");
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
How it works:
When the user clicks Click Me, the showMessage() function runs and displays:

Welcome to JavaScript!
Q22. Demonstrate event-driven programming
Answer:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Event Driven Programming</title>
</head>
<body>

    <button id="myBtn">Click Me</button>

    <p id="demo">Original text</p>

    <script>
        document.getElementById("myBtn").addEventListener("click", function() {
            document.getElementById("demo").textContent = "Button was clicked!";
        });
    </script>

</body>
</html>
Explanation:
The click is an event. When the button is clicked, JavaScript responds to that event and changes the paragraph text.

This is called event-driven programming. 


